.TH CRC32 1
.SH NAME
crc32 \- compute a CRC-32 checksum, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crc32
[
.BI -x\  xorval
]
[
.BI -i\  initial
]
[
.BI -p\  poly
]
[
.I file
\&...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Crc32
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's real
.IR crc32 (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/crc32.c ,
99 lines in the 9front tree), not a behavioral workalike \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
With no arguments,
.I crc32
computes the standard CRC-32 (the one used by gzip, zip, and Ethernet)
of standard input and prints it in hex, followed by a newline. With one
or more
.I file
arguments, it computes and prints the checksum of each in turn, each
line followed by a tab and the file's name; a file that cannot be
opened or read is reported to standard error and skipped, but does not
stop the remaining files from being processed \(em the overall failure
is only signalled once, after everything has been attempted.
.SS Options
.TP
.BI -x\  xorval
Value XORed with the running checksum both before the first byte and
after the last (default all-ones \(em the standard CRC-32's final
complement).
.TP
.BI -i\  initial
Initial register value before
.B -x
is applied (default 0).
.TP
.BI -p\  poly
The (reflected) generator polynomial (default
.BR 0xedb88320 ,
the standard CRC-32/zlib/PKZIP/Ethernet polynomial).
.PP
Each of
.BR -x / -i / -p 's
values may be decimal, or (following the original's own
.B strtol
base-0 convention) hex with a leading
.B 0x
or octal with a leading
.BR 0 .
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/crc32.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/crc32.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR crc32test (1)
.SH BUGS
An unrecognised option is a usage error here, unlike
.IR join (1),
whose original silently ignores one \(em a genuine difference between
the two originals' own conventions, not unified away by this port.
.PP
The original's
.B u32int
(32-bit unsigned) arithmetic, including a right shift that must be
logical rather than sign-extending, is carried as Limbo
.B big
masked to 32 bits throughout \(em Limbo's own
.B int
right-shift sign-extends, which would corrupt this algorithm outright;
see
.BR crc32.b 's
header comment for why a masked, always-non-negative
.B big
sidesteps the problem without needing a separate logical-shift
operator (Limbo has none for a signed type).
.PP
.BR strtol 's
own edge cases (leading whitespace, overflow, an explicit sign) are
not reproduced bit-for-bit, only its base-0 hex/octal/decimal auto-
detection \(em unlike
.IR join (1),
there is no known real divergence in ordinary use of this option
worth preserving here.
